#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256678 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256679 :question/problem "![](images/34/34aca579b2890586365298c764d051f57d4c80977ee975caad505c923343f90d.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256680 :question/problem "![](images/77/77f88ec1dfbd85b4609800484963d2480e68009eb4ac1f6c9fb620914633ff2a.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256681 :question/problem "![](images/a0/a032eb49f78ce7bd54de6969f030e4316f2bd3028280f73991af3fe45521733c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256682 :question/problem "![](images/25/251e49513353092b4f9a16f54bb14fbaa1afe1a14e5fecf5b3ce54905fed474d.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256683 :question/problem "![](images/d1/d13f8c3fdea65c5f05df12585aec7e18564bdbe390ebb895b8b2d01dda74708a.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ component) is zero for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is positive for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256684 :question/problem "![](images/72/721252c30370d98fe776ca00e43fef9c95a5a074140dfbdf2a00be19a66b4a1e.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is zero for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ component) is negative for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=-5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256685 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne -2,\\,x-y\\ge 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $U(x,y)=⟨\\frac{1}{x+2},\\sqrt{x-y}⟩$; $V(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256686 :question/problem "![](images/7c/7cb4dd61f9d42a48c022a183e7f4310a8a4ed7ae881edd136881ef6863282e2c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256687 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A(x,y)$ $=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256688 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}(x-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y),\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-y>0\\,⟹\\,x>y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}x>y\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}x>y\\}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256689 :question/problem "![](images/f8/f89653698d9c2bad0c2e6a77d4dad1953a9fd72f89cbfc08fd68aac802acd284.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{j}$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{j}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256690 :question/problem "![](images/64/64f02fa7dcd23b20e61801c0453d8fe7ed013936572e1ee63f748361edd4dec5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{k}$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{k}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256691 :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/9f/9f5f11a4ca31cb796f5a95a1541aa946e24fd1b7079ab8069e5743d41d92d764.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](images/5b/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/95/95e8d137f90135de1727451fbc96ddfcc325e9e4461054615c91743a4a9ea54a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256693 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne y}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $U(x,y)=⟨(x+2){e}^{y},\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-y}⟩$; $W(x,y)=⟨x-3{y}^{2},2-y⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256694 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $U(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $T(x,y,z)$ $=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i$ $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ $+{z}^{2}\\,k$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256695 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $D(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $G(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,y-z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $D(x,y,z)$ $=5\\,i$ $+{z}^{2}\\,j$ $+(y+x)\\,k$; $G(x,y,z)$ $=x(y+1)\\,i$ $+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y-z)\\,j$ $+{z}^{3}\\,k$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256696 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $V(x,y,z)=⟨5{e}^{-{z}^{2}},{x}^{3}y,1-{y}^{3}⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256697 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =-\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i+(-1)j=2\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/36/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256698 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $P(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,3-x\\ge 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,2x-y+z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P(x,y,z)$ $=⟨{e}^{x-2y},\\sqrt{3-x},3{z}^{3}⟩$; $Q(x,y,z)$ $=⟨-1,2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-y+z)⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256699 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne y,\\,z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $E(x,y,z)$ $=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+z),\\operatorname{tan}⁡z,\\frac{1}{x-y}⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256700 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{3t}+1,\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3\\cdot 0}+1=1+1=2, \\\\ y & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)),\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))}{3{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)}{3{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)}{3{e}^{2\\cdot 0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,0)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ is the $y$-intercept."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256701 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=2y-x?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =-i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/92/92f46798ebeae9017c91ddae24e0f68cef81a1045314950bc4b56af003af0b42.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256704 :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}(1-{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\sqrt{1-|y|}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=x\\sqrt{1-|y|}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,-1<x<1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/89/89c78ec587826c15c90ef4ffdfc0fd9f8af3d7b4d3d1cc099ce24b883b9842c2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-|y|\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,-1\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/ad/addd649aa739711fd34ebd3e82579c8c0100a2b9a109d5cafabde750bc94eb9a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/49/49000dd6905017bc64c9a67b6d8a2ea475a684477ac6e02d6ed15c4c7dbbb3aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256705 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(0,-2),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+1}\\,j=i-j \\\\ ∇f(0,-2) & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)}{(0)^{2}(-2)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{(0)^{2}(-2)^{2}+1}\\,j=-4\\,i \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{(2)^{2}(0)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{(2)^{2}(0)^{2}+1}\\,j=4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1d/1da33de6ab53eee30cb7dbdba61a437fd9e3209437d061d2c546386981962942.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256706 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4{t}^{5},\\,y=9t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=20{t}^{4},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=9.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n20{t}^{4}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}^{′}(0)=9\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256708 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2(y-2x) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =y-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2(y-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,1),$ $(1,3),$ and $(-2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1) & =-2\\cdot (1-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(1-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,3) & =-2\\cdot (3-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(3-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=-2\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3) & =-2\\cdot (-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,i+(-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,j=-2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/25/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256709 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),(1,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-1,1) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,i+(-1)(1)\\,j=-3\\,i-j \\\\ F(1,2) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,i+(1)(2)\\,j=3\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,-1) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\,i+(2)(-1)\\,j=-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/5e/5e9e75b66969e1acd7b1a90a5243a3a496168644a53d541851ba933a5f465553.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256710 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{2}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-4,2),(-1,1),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{2}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-4,2) & =-2(-4)\\,i+{2}^{2}\\,j=8\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ F(-1,1) & =-2(-1)\\,i+{2}^{1}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,0) & =-2(2)\\,i+{2}^{0}\\,j=-4\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/58/58a106f2c0ac7aacd30f7ba8915fd2317a67efb73c1c48fe2d192a5d2576717a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256712 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\frac{x}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-|x|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-3,1),(2,-1),$ and $(4,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\frac{x}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-|x|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-3,1) & =-\\left(\\frac{-3}{1}\\right)i-|-3|\\,j=3\\,i-3\\,j \\\\ F(2,-1) & =-\\left(\\frac{2}{-1}\\right)i-|2|\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(4,2) & =-\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right)i-|4|\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/33/330bf1e0b9b2dff008a3e3878d5b2acba8e14addb637d637874492220cb08f2c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256713 :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a46390ced6cc52c535f9f48e5e157cea83960caa339e98b23e3b64c93e0ea38.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is a line segment directed along the $y$-axis. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3-(-1)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256714 :question/problem "![](images/d8/d82f445e19af6e6d343bfab6c082108e945a8feb21caa9b60a2d93da24fc3f6a.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the line segment directed along the $x$-axis. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=5-1=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256715 :question/problem "![](images/e4/e41579712730d26c66d9f46f8d978fd51c8efbd76394c094113454e24b3aaff7.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $2$ and $4:$ \n\n![](images/84/849f44d06de55f14811d433f9ccfdd30d47f6a1db1970cdfbfbd6f16be4959b0.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256716 :question/problem "![](images/69/69072873cb1f613ee9c2fa4a4caf3dc3409026c2a8310847b1c3dd890cc816d8.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $6:$ \n\n![](images/d0/d0d9f0fad038f04b6d93920979161694f4e709a31b68083f20e768bf60aa1675.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3\\sqrt{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256718 :question/problem "![](images/20/201804549f48637076fcaad5252632527b937e01cc75c153bc1db26b246d2aa6.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/f4/f4dc7eaf7ed7651ee38157c486f185ef3eecaf9ccb7e91df18e897c56c5c5e23.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/9c/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256720 :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=-1,$ which gives the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=0,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=2,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/77/77a54ef31c41e0246e3d337a69c7faa161194058e6a5c672ef24d86866a83faf.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move downward and away from the origin $O.$ \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/4e/4e124d5e77ef9a28002aba9aac40f0b7eff1232dd1c08b6bca10b166469675c7.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n \n\n![](images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256721 :question/problem "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)=⟨{t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 2.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-4\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4\n$$ is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=8,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨16{u}^{4}-12{u}^{2},\\,2u⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$” is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx={y}^{4}-3{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256722 :question/problem "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(u)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)⟩\n$$ for $0\\le u\\le 2\\pi .$ \n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=2,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le \\pi .$” is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we note that both parameterizations traverse a half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ between $0$ and $\\pi .$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-1\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1\n$$ is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256723 :question/problem "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 4.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-3\n$$ is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=12,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨4u,\\,8{u}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$” is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3/2},\\,0\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256725 :question/problem "![](images/39/39a7cb0b31cb5d906803063597ae968be8fba90f048a4d9745225de7c3f8d6db.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/05/058c7b33112329df321e61660df4f2d388f42ca40c0aae5b73a089a0251726e2.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe paths ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are line segments of length $3.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3+5+3=11.\n$$"}]}
